{"url": "https://restofworld.org/2022/indonesia-hacked-sim-bjorka", "title": "Sick of data leaks, Indonesians are siding with a hacker who exposed 1.3 billion SIM card details", "full_content": "Politics\nSick of data leaks, Indonesians are siding with a hacker who exposed 1.3 billion SIM card details\nBreaches are so common in Indonesia that citizens jokingly call it an “open-source country.”\n\nGarry Lotulung/Anadolu Agency/Getty Images\n\nGarry Lotulung/Anadolu Agency/Getty Images\n\nBy\nAntonia Timmerman\n\n5 October 2022\n\nOn August 31, a user named Bjorka posted an entry on a little-known site called\nBreached Forums\nIndonesians woke up to the breach in confusion, which quickly turned into anger. The Ministry of Communication and Information Technology, or Kominfo, responded by telling citizens they were responsible for regularly changing their passwords; popular meme accounts\nreposted\npleaded haplessly with Bjorka at a press conference:\nIndonesians’ data is exposed at such a rate that citizens jokingly call it an “open-source country.”\nDigital rights group\nSafenet\nbiggest-ever data breach case in Asia\n“open-source country.”\nIn 2020, leaks from companies, including e-commerce giants Tokopedia and Bukalapak, exposed over\n100 million users’ personal data\nbreached\nAfter years of increasingly brazen leaks, frustration among Indonesians is reaching a boiling point — enough to fuel the hasty passage of a\nlong-delayed personal data protection bill\ndirected to hunt down the hacker Bjorka.\nIn a twist, many Indonesians\nhave even sided with the hacker\nexpose sloppy data governance\nreportedly\n“In 2018, [Kominfo] forced us to register for phone numbers using [government ID], promising us to be free of spam,” cybersecurity consultant Teguh Aprianto\npointed out on Twitter\nKominfo and the National Cyber and Crypto Agency (BSSN) did not respond to requests for comment.\n\nA user named Bjorka posted advertisements for the stolen data on Breached Forums.\nbreached.to\nMaryam Jameelah, a lecturer in Malang, East Java, admitted feeling traumatized when reading about the recent data breach cases on the news. Two years ago, Jameelah was one of the victims from the Tokopedia data leak, and for months, would receive bills from transactions she’d never made.\n“I had to change my number and all of my accounts,” Jameelah told\nRest of World\nMulyadi, an IT auditor at a Big Four firm, told\nRest of World\nDespite the passage of the personal data protection bill, which details criminal sanctions for data handlers and corporations in case of a data leak, experts say the new measures are temporary, designed to cool Indonesians’ rage.\n“It depends on who is on the team [of the taskforce]. Do they have competence?” Ismail Fahmi, founder of media tracker Drone Emprit, told\nRest of World\nAt the heart of the issue is a patchwork approach to data security. Companies must share customer data with the Ministry of Internal Affairs to verify their identities, a government official told\nRest of World\n\nExplore charts >>\n\nThese state departments are also supposed to work together. But coordination is lacking, while leaks are rampant. Kominfo, for instance, presides over communication, information, and internet laws; BSSN is tasked with improving the system against hacks; and the police’s cyber crime unit is set up to enforce\ncyber crime-related laws\nThe government official explained to\nRest of World\n“Unfortunately, [state departments] almost never share their investigation results with Kominfo, so the ministry is often forced to offer a recommendation only based on compliance information,” which constitutes basic security standards, the official said.\nMuch of Indonesians’ hopes now seem to hinge on the personal data protection bill and the subsequent new body of authority that will be created. The president will have the prerogative right to decide who will make up the new institution.\nWahyudi Djafar, executive director in the Institute for Policy Research and Advocacy (ELSAM), told\nRest of World\n“The challenge is how strong this institution’s authority will be, [which] will entirely depend on the president’s good faith,” Djafar added.\nAntonia Timmerman", "source_type": "news", "sector": "government", "language": "en", "publication_date": "2022-10-05", "collected_at": "2026-06-03T06:44:01.944358+00:00", "content_hash": "ff1d4bca2535bf61a845d22133ae6a46fa458c87", "dup_group": "", "is_canonical": true}
