{"url": "https://cyberpress.org/data-breach-in-indonesia", "title": "Data Breach in Indonesia Exposes 7.5 Million Records", "full_content": "Data Breach in Indonesia Exposes 7.5 Million Records\n\nBy\nAnuPriya\n\nFebruary 14, 2025\n\nCategories:\n\nA data breach has rocked Indonesia, with 7.5 million records from the South Jakarta ward database allegedly leaked on a dark web forum.\nThe breach underscores persistent vulnerabilities in the nation’s cybersecurity infrastructure and raises pressing concerns about data protection.\nDetails of the Alleged Breach\nAccording to the post from Cyberfeeddigest, the breach reportedly targeted the website of South Jakarta’s local government, selatan.jakarta.go.id, exposing sensitive personal information.\nThe compromised data includes ID numbers, residential addresses, employment records, health details, and education information.\nThese details are now circulating online, with links for downloading the data being shared widely.\nCybersecurity experts warn that such sensitive data could be exploited for\nidentity theft\nThe exposure to health and social status information also raises ethical concerns about how public institutions manage citizen data.\nWhile the authenticity of the leak remains unverified, its potential scale has alarmed both citizens and experts.\nImplications for Indonesia’s Cybersecurity\nThis incident is part of a troubling trend of cyberattacks in Indonesia, which has seen multiple high-profile\nbreaches\nFrom voter databases to government systems, hackers have repeatedly exploited weaknesses in digital infrastructure.\nFor instance, a similar breach in 2024 exposed millions of civil service records on the dark web.\nExperts attribute these vulnerabilities to insufficient investment in cybersecurity and weak enforcement of existing data protection laws.\nAlthough Indonesia enacted its Personal Data Protection Law (PDP Law) in 2022, its implementation has been slow and inconsistent.\nThe current breach highlights systemic issues that need urgent attention.\nWithout robust measures to secure sensitive databases, incidents like these will continue to undermine public trust and expose millions to financial and personal risks.\nGovernment Response and Public Outcry\nAs of now, there has been no official confirmation or denial from the South Jakarta government regarding the breach. However, public pressure is mounting for transparency and accountability.\nCitizens have expressed frustration over repeated failures to safeguard their personal information.\nThe Indonesian government has been criticized for its reactive approach to cybersecurity incidents.\nExperts suggest that proactive measures—such as regular security audits, stricter enforcement of\ndata protection\nIn response to similar breaches in the past, authorities have worked with cybersecurity agencies to investigate and mitigate risks.\nHowever, the lack of timely communication often exacerbates public concerns.\nThis latest incident serves as a stark reminder of the urgent need for comprehensive reforms to strengthen Indonesia’s digital governance framework.\nThis breach not only exposes millions of citizens to potential harm but also highlights broader challenges in securing digital infrastructure in an increasingly connected world.\nAs Indonesia continues its digital transformation journey, prioritizing cybersecurity will be critical for safeguarding public trust and ensuring sustainable progress.\nAlso Read:\n\nSecurity Risks Emerge with Windows 11’s New Compression Formats in libarchive\n\nShare\nFacebook\nTwitter\nPinterest\nWhatsApp\n\nAnuPriya\nAny Priya is a cybersecurity reporter at Cyber Press, specializing in cyber attacks, dark web monitoring, data breaches, vulnerabilities, and malware. She delivers in-depth analysis on emerging threats and digital security trends.\n\nRecent Articles\n\nRussia Reports Foreign Spyware Found on Officials’ Mobile Phones\n\nCyber Security News\n\nJune 2, 2026\n\nClaude Code GitHub Actions Flaw Enables Repository Compromise\n\nCyber Security News\n\nJune 2, 2026\n\nSolyxImmortal Python Malware Steals Browser Passwords and Cookies\n\nMalware\n\nJune 2, 2026\n\nAZUREVEIL Adaptix C2 Agent Delivered via Targeted Spearphishing Attacks\n\nCyber Security News\n\nJune 2, 2026\n\nCritical KMW CCTV Flaw Enables Unauthorized Camera Access\n\nCyber Security News\n\nJune 2, 2026\n\nRelated Stories\n\nCyber Security News\nRussia Reports Foreign Spyware Found on Officials’ Mobile Phones\n\nLucas Martin\n-\nJune 2, 2026\n\nCyber Security News\nClaude Code GitHub Actions Flaw Enables Repository Compromise\n\nLucas Martin\n-\nJune 2, 2026\n\nCyber Security News\nAZUREVEIL Adaptix C2 Agent Delivered via Targeted Spearphishing Attacks\n\nVarshini\n-\nJune 2, 2026\n\nCyber Security News\nCritical KMW CCTV Flaw Enables Unauthorized Camera Access\n\nLucas Martin\n-\nJune 2, 2026\n\nCyber Security News\nCISA Flags Palo Alto PAN-OS Flaw as Actively Exploited\n\nLucas Martin\n-\nJune 2, 2026\n\nCyber Security News\nAndroid 0-Day Vulnerability Exploited for Full Device Control\n\nLucas Martin\n-\nJune 2, 2026\n\nLEAVE A REPLY\nCancel reply\n\nComment:\nPlease enter your comment!\n\nName:*\nPlease enter your name here\n\nEmail:*\nYou have entered an incorrect email address!\nPlease enter your email address here\n\nWebsite:\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment.", "source_type": "news", "sector": "government", "language": "en", "publication_date": "2025-02-14", "collected_at": "2026-06-03T06:44:39.840478+00:00", "content_hash": "95b905906152867c39c968002084b1a10196001b", "dup_group": "", "is_canonical": true}
